  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They didn't need early magic. J.T. Ginn and the bullpen locked the door, and the lineup cashed in when it mattered.",
    "what_this_means": "Ginn delivered six clean innings against the Dodgers, and the bullpen did not let air out. Luis Medina threw 1.2 scoreless with three strikeouts, and Hogan Harris added 1.1 more without a blemish. That was the structure. The offense built on it in the fourth and fifth. Two runs in the fourth to break through, three more in the fifth to make it final. Jonah Heim supplied the power with two home runs and drove in two when runners were on base. This is the shape that wins games in July: the starter holds it tight, the pen keeps it sealed, and the lineup converts runners into runs. The Dodgers never answered once the Athletics took control. Three left on base is nothing. They scored on the chances that mattered.",
    "what_to_watch": "Down 3.5 games in the division, every win pulls them closer. Miami arrives tonight at 6:40 with Tyler Phillips on the mound. Phillips sits at a 3.02 ERA. Sharp command, low-velocity stuff. The Athletics' young lineup has seen Phillips twice this season and has not found much. If Jack Perkins can keep pace, this becomes a game where late contact could matter.",
